"""Session 3. Measuring a model: accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC and AUC, thresholds, baselines, overfitting. A technical session (v3, 6 November 2026; terms and examples revised 6 November 2026).

Teaching order: a smoke alarm (invented illustration numbers: 1,000 kitchen events, 20 real fires) carries the confusion matrix, precision, recall, accuracy, the majority-class baseline,
the threshold and F1; a spam filter carries overfitting. Then the late-invoice data are the application.
Every invoice number on the slides comes from the recorded run in demo_runs/s03/ (s03_measure.py, results.json) and demo_runs/s03_measuring_a_model.md:
a fictional Nile Valley Foods late-payment dataset of 3,000 invoices, fixed seeds, scikit-learn 1.9.1, 900 held-back test invoices of which 83 were paid late.
The assumed costs (UGX 20,000 to chase a flag, UGX 300,000 for a missed late invoice) are an assumption for the lesson, and the slides say so.
The smoke alarm numbers: 1,000 events, 20 fires, alarm rang 50 times: TP 16, FP 34, FN 4, TN 946. Precision 32%, recall 80%, accuracy 96.2%, F1 46%. Never ringing: accuracy 98%, recall 0%.
Other alarm settings: high threshold rang 20 times (TP 12, FP 8, FN 8); low threshold rang 200 times (TP 19, FP 181, FN 1).
"""

